  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1118 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows our next trough digging towards the\nRockies tonight. This will support increasing lee troughing east\nof the Rockies and a low-level jet over the Plains. Off the \nhigher elevations to the west and on the nose of the low-level \njet, showers and thunderstorms have begun to develop across\nportions of the Plains. With the better forcing displaced north\nand west of the area, the majority of this activity should miss\nus. That said, a stray shower or thunderstorm may clip \nnortheast Nebraska and west-central Iowa tonight. With \nincreasing winds and cloud cover, temperatures hold in the lower\n70s through Saturday morning. \n\nSaturday morning into the afternoon sees increasing southwest \nwinds as the pressure gradient increases between the building \nridge and approaching trough. Wind speeds may reach as high as \n20-30+ mph making for a breezy afternoon. These winds should \nalso help fuel our hot temperatures. Widespread mid to upper 90s\noverspread the region with heat index values near 100. Wind \nspeeds decrease slightly Saturday night, but these winds and any\nclouds should be enough to keep temperatures in the mid 70s \nthrough Sunday morning. \n\nWhile the main trough remains northwest of the area, a leading \nshortwave is forecast to eject across the Plains on Sunday. \nThis shortwave has a low chance of developing a few showers and \nthunderstorms across the region late Sunday morning into the \nafternoon. Any showers or thunderstorms may pack a punch with \nstrong synoptic winds and a relatively dry sub-cloud layer \nhelping to produce strong downdrafts. A few strong to severe \nwind gusts may be possible if a stronger thunderstorm can form. \nWhile rain chances and clouds provides some uncertainty for the \ntemperatures forecast, it will remain hot. Temperatures will be \nsimilar to or even higher than on Saturday with heat index \nvalues now approaching 105 across southeast Nebraska. A heat \nadvisory may be needed due to this heat. The breezy conditions \nand lingering clouds will result in a warm start to the week \nwith mid to upper 70s to start the day on Monday.\n\nNext week will see the heat ease somewhat. The main trough that\nbrought us our weekend winds and weather will move off towards \nthe northeast on Monday. This will push a \"cold front\" into the\narea. The front shaves several degrees off of high and low \ntemperatures, but by no means brings actual cooler weather. In \nreality, temperatures remain above normal despite this front. \nThese above normal temperatures hold steady through much of next\nweek. Periodic thunderstorm chances also return to the \nforecast, though day to day rain chances remain low. This \nweather pattern is supported by a relatively zonal upper-level \npattern across the central and northern Plains after the \nweekend trough. Hopefully, more relief from the heat arrives as \nwe move into the month of September.",
